<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pennsylvania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pennsylvania is a state in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It was founded in 1681 when Charles II granted the territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a>, who made it a refuge for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> and other religious groups. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, its main city, was where the Declaration of Independence and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a> were adopted, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gettysburg">Battle of Gettysburg</a> was fought in the state in 1863.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pennsylvania.jpg" alt="Pennsylvania" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simtropolitan, Jean-Pierre Demailly (old version) permission= Insignia This image shows a flag, a coat of arms, a sea... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pennsylvania.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Repeated wars in the seventeenth century devastated the land, and in the eighteenth century poverty and religious pressure drove thousands of Palatines to emigrate, many to <b>Pennsylvania</b> and New York, where they formed a large part of the Pennsylvania Dutch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1688 residents of Germantown, <b>Pennsylvania</b>, issued the first organised protest against slavery in the English colonies, and in the 18th century John Woolman and Anthony Benezet persuaded the Society of Friends to expel members who traded in slaves.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>James Wilson of <b>Pennsylvania</b> argued for a single executive chosen by the people, holding that one person would be more energetic and more clearly accountable than a council, while Edmund Randolph warned that a single executive would be “the foetus of monarchy”.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…Coverdale made the first translation in 1539; Frederick H. Hedge's of 1853 and Thomas Carlyle's became the most popular outside Lutheranism, while North American Lutherans long sang a composite translation from the 1868 <b>Pennsylvania</b> Lutheran Church Book and, more recently, that of the 1978 Lutheran Book of Worship.</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-mighty-fortress-is-our-god">A Mighty Fortress Is Our God</a></li><li><blockquote>Fragments were found among the tablets that the University of <b>Pennsylvania</b> Museum excavated from the temple library at Nippur in present-day Iraq.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kesh-temple-hymn">Kesh Temple Hymn</a></li><li><blockquote>Its first lines were found among tablets from the temple library at Nippur in the University of <b>Pennsylvania</b> Museum; George Barton translated them in 1918 as &quot;A prayer for the city of Ur&quot;, noting that it was plausible but unproven that the prayer was written in the last…</blockquote><a href="/songs/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a></li></ul></section>
